{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Interchange the order of integration
 and summing, obtaining }}
}
.EQN 6 8 227 0
{0:Z1}NAME({0:\w}NAME):(1)/(2*{0:\p}NAME)*(0&2*{0:\p}NAME`{0:X1}NAME({0:u}NAME)*{0:n}NAME$({0:e}NAME)^(-1i*({0:\w}NAME-{0:u}NAME)*{0:n}NAME)*({0:w}NAME)[({0:n}NAME)&{0:u}NAME)
.TXT 9 -8 228 0
Cg a61.875000,61.750000,217
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We}{ recognize the DTFT of w}{\fs16 
\dn6 n}{ with argument (}{\f1 w}{-u) and
 so the property is established.\par The
 convolution is a periodic convolution as
 both W1(}{\f1 w}{) and X1(}{\f1 w}{) have
 period 2}{\f1 p}{.}}
}
.TXT 6 0 229 0
Cg a62.250000,61.875000,981
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We make an important application
 of Property 7.8. Let x}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{ 
represent an arbitrarily long data sequence
 with DTFT X1(}{\f1 w}{). Suppose the latter
 is unknown and we wish to compute it from
 an observation of x}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{. In
 practice we can only observe a finite-length
 segment of x}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{, namely the
 }{\b truncated}{ data z}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{
 = w}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{ x}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{ .
 The finite-length sequence w}{\fs16 \dn6 
n}{ is called a }{\b window}{ sequence. 
Given the finite-length sequence z}{\fs16 
\dn6 n}{, we compute its DFT: with the zero-padding
 technique, we can thereby obtain samples
 of the DTFT Z1(}{\f1 w}{) to whatever detail
 we desire. However, Z1(}{\f1 w}{) is }{\ul 
not}{ X1(}{\f1 w}{): rather, it is the result
 of the convolution described by Property
 7.8. It is appropriate to term X1(}{\f1 
w}{) an }{\b idealized}{ }{\b model}{, because
 it is defined by a sequence, x}{\fs16 \dn6 
n}{ , of infinite length and hence unobservable.
 }}
}
.TXT 25 0 230 0
Cg a62.125000,61.750000,273
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {This leads us directly to pose 
the problem of choosing a window w}{\fs16 
\dn6 n}{ with a good DTFT W1. }{The window
 is not necessarily of constant value. In
 fact, we usually want a nonconstant window
 as we will discuss later in connection 
with this measurement }{ }{   }}
}
