.TXT 34 2 249 0
Cg a60.750000,65.000000,85
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We see that the truncation can 
make inferences about X1 from Z1 quite problematic.}}
}
.TXT 4 0 250 0
Cg a61.500000,61.875000,470
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b Exercise 7.3}{  Vary the parameters
 in Example 7.1 and observe Z1. For example,
 vary M with the other parameters fixed.
 Then try markedly different amplitudes 
on the cosine sequences. Notice that the
 window can be varied only by changing its
 length, M: this changes its DTFT's  }{\b 
main lobe width}{, defined as the distance
 between zeros enclosing the maximum, but
 it does not change the relative heights
 of the subsidiary maxima, or }{\b sidelobe
 levels}{.}}
}
.TXT 15 0 251 0
Cg a61.750000,61.750000,442
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b Problem 7.3}{  Experience with
 Exercise 7.4 suggests that it is of interest
 to consider a nonconstant sequence for 
a window. Recall that the FIR for the }{\ul 
\b 5 by 7}{ filter has a tapered shape that
 results }{in a DTFT with}{ much lower sidelobe
 level, albeit at the cost of a broader 
mainlobe width. Suppose we use a fairly 
pronounced taper, resulting in a triangular
 shaped window, in place of the rectangularly
 shaped window.}}
}
.ATT
.STATUS Go to Section 6, p. 4
.GOTO 6_bk.mcd
.ATT_END
