.TXT 6 -10 256 0
Cg a24.750000,70.000000,48
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b \fs24 In}{\b \fs24 finitely 
Long Sequences}}
}
.TXT 4 10 257 0
Cg a62.125000,61.875000,519
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {It can be useful to consider the
 DTFT of sequences of unlimited length. 
While not observable, they can be useful
 as idealized models. Their mathematical
 analysis may be easier than that for finite-length
 signals- with the significant proviso that
 we have developed the needed theory and
 manipulative skills. In fact, numerous 
theories of Fourier transforms can be established.
 Such a discussion would draw us far afield
 and we will be content here to formally
 develop only what we shall need in our 
DSP study. }}
}
.TXT 16 0 258 0
Cg a59.125000,64.000000,166
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {First, note that the DTFT of a 
sequence x}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{ will exist provided
 that x}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{ is absolutely summable.
 This is established by the simple bound}}
}
.EQN 7 10 259 0
|({0:X1}NAME({0:\w}NAME))÷|({0:n}NAME$({0:e}NAME)^(-1i*{0:\w}NAME*{0:n}NAME)*({0:x}NAME)[({0:n}NAME))ó{0:n}NAME$|(({0:x}NAME)[({0:n}NAME))
.TXT 7 -10 260 0
Cg a52.250000,64.000000,82
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {It may be shown that X1(}{\f1 w}{)
 for such a sequence is uniformly continuous.}}
}
.TXT 3 0 262 0
Cg a62.375000,62.000000,256
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {This theory is adequate to handle
 all practical cases but not all idealized
 models that may be of interest to us. The
 glaring omission is the fundamentally important
 complex exponential sequence! We show how
 to formally include it in our discussions.
 }}
}
